# Blog readability — fun, skimmable, well-titled

Blog posts should feel inviting to read, not like a dense book chapter. A reader should be able to **skim headings and callouts** and still get the story.

Reference implementation: `src/components/blog/WhistlerWeatherForecastContent.tsx`

## Structure

- **Lead hook** — one strong opening paragraph (larger type, `not-prose`) that states the topic and why it matters.
- **SectionHeader** — major sections with optional kicker labels (`Start here`, `Plan your trip`, `Bottom line`). Use `id` for TOC anchors.
- **SubSectionTitle** — h3-style breaks inside long sections so no section runs more than ~4 paragraphs without a sub-heading.
- **TextBlock** — group related paragraphs in `<div className="space-y-4">`. Never use one sentence per `<p>` across the whole article.

## Callouts (break up walls of text)

- **KeyPoints** — bullet lists for stats, takeaways, or “what to expect” summaries.
- **StatCallout** — single standout stat or quote with left border emphasis.
- **OutlookCard** (or similar) — structured metadata blocks (outlook, highlights, confidence).
- **At-a-glance box** — gray bordered summary near the top for scanners.

## Writing style

- Merge choppy one-liners into full paragraphs (2–4 sentences each).
- Use kickers and sub-titles that sound human, not robotic (`Our early prediction`, not `Summary`).
- Alternate prose → callout → prose → image so the rhythm stays visual.
- Keep FAQ, CTAs, and images **outside** `TextBlock` wrappers — never nest section headers inside text blocks.

## Avoid

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — book-like, impossible to skim
<p>El Niño is here.</p>
<p>It is strengthening.</p>
<p>NOAA says 90% chance.</p>
<p>That means warmer weather.</p>

// ✅ GOOD — grouped prose + scannable callout
<TextBlock>
  <p>El Niño is here and strengthening. NOAA gives a greater than 90% chance of a very strong event this winter — expect a warmer, more variable season.</p>
</TextBlock>
<KeyPoints title="Historical highlights" items={["January: 138% of normal", "..."]} />
```

When adding or rewriting blog content, apply these patterns even if the layout helpers live in the same file as the article (co-locate small helpers like the weather forecast post does).
